{
  "image_name": "abfec03e5743cfb9b347e56d08f8a5e6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes interfacial design strategies in solid-state batteries, especially how solid electrolytes interact with cathodes and lithium metal anodes. A main theme is that coating or buffering the interface can convert poor, point-like contact into a larger, more continuous contact area, which improves lithium-ion transport and forms a more favorable interphase. For cathodes, a solid-electrolyte coating around active material particles appears to create connected ion-migration pathways and a stable interfacial layer, while composite cathodes mix active material, electrolyte, and conductive additive so that ions and electrons can reach the reaction sites more effectively.\n\nFor lithium metal, the figure contrasts different solid electrolytes by the kind of interface they generate. Some sulfide or halide-type materials seem to form mixed conductive interphases that allow both ions and electrons, promoting unwanted lithium plating/stripping behavior and rapid interphase growth. In contrast, other interphases are shown as lithium-ion conductive but electronically blocking, which helps suppress parasitic reactions. The final panels show a bilayer or buffer approach at the lithium interface: adding an interfacial layer improves wetting, reduces void formation and local current hotspots, and mitigates dendrite growth by stabilizing contact during cycling.",
